from course import p,ul,code,note,step,refs

modules=[]
def add(slug,title,prereq,run,intro,steps,proof,questions,fix,links):
    modules.append(dict(slug=slug,title=title,prereq=prereq,run=run,intro=intro,steps=steps,proof=proof,questions=questions,fix=fix,links=links))

add('ext-01-case','E1 · Prepare a shared investigation and review network','Core Weeks 1–14. No Elastic, Splunk, or Security Onion experience required.',
'Host only for case preparation; then one new Ubuntu guest at a time. Keep original course VMs off.',
'Different products should lead to defensible conclusions from the same evidence. CASE-M01 is deliberately synthetic: it combines a short sequence of failed logins, ordinary web requests, process records, and a file change. The packet capture contains only its four HTTP transactions. It cannot corroborate the login or Windows records.',[
step('1. Download and preserve the case',p('Download <a href="case-m01.zip">CASE-M01</a> to C:\\CyberLab\\Evidence. In File Explorer right-click → Extract All. Confirm the extracted case-m01 folder contains events.ndjson, events.bulk.ndjson, auth-input.ndjson, auth-bad.ndjson, http-access.log, web-case.pcap, README.txt, SHA256SUMS, and mentor-answer-key.json. Read README first; leave the answer key closed until you have attempted the questions.')+code('Get-FileHash C:\\CyberLab\\Evidence\\case-m01\\events.ndjson -Algorithm SHA256\nGet-FileHash C:\\CyberLab\\Evidence\\case-m01\\web-case.pcap -Algorithm SHA256','HOST • PowerShell; adjust only if extraction created an extra folder')+p('Compare with SHA256SUMS. A matching hash proves unchanged bytes relative to this supplied manifest, not independent provenance. Preserve the original ZIP and do exercises on copies. Do not change timestamps to make old events appear in a “last 15 minutes” search.')),
step('2. Inspect the format before importing',code('$caseRows = Get-Content C:\\CyberLab\\Evidence\\case-m01\\events.ndjson | ForEach-Object { $_ | ConvertFrom-Json }\n$caseRows.Count\n$caseRows | Group-Object { $_.event.dataset } | Select-Object Name,Count\n$caseRows | Select-Object -First 1 | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 8','HOST • PowerShell')+p('NDJSON means one complete JSON object per line. There are 16 normalized events across four sources. event.id identifies an event; labels.case_id ties the story together. @timestamp is event time in UTC. These fields are inspired by common security schemas, but this is not a full ECS or Splunk CIM dataset and it is not native Sysmon or Wazuh output.')),
step('3. Write hypotheses and negative tests',p('Before opening any SIEM, answer: Which user had repeated failures? Was there a later success? Did /private.txt return content? Was the Windows command destructive? Write “unknown” where evidence is insufficient. Define four queries: failed logins by user/source; all HTTP statuses; the CYBERLAB-M01 command marker; and a non-existent user that must return zero. Record the fixed search interval: 2026-09-01 09:59:00 through 10:03:00 UTC.')),
step('4. Build a separate host-accessible review network',p('The original CyberLab-Private network remains for live exercises. This new Internal switch connects the host to review guests for SSH and file transfer; it is not equivalent to the private isolation boundary. In administrator PowerShell inspect existing interfaces and routes. If 10.78.0.0/24 overlaps a real network or VPN, stop and agree a different unused subnet with the mentor, then consistently replace it in these extension instructions.')+code('Get-VMSwitch | Format-Table Name,SwitchType\nGet-NetIPAddress -AddressFamily IPv4 | Format-Table InterfaceAlias,IPAddress,PrefixLength\nGet-NetRoute -AddressFamily IPv4 | Format-Table DestinationPrefix,InterfaceAlias\nNew-VMSwitch -Name CyberLab-Review -SwitchType Internal\nNew-NetIPAddress -InterfaceAlias "vEthernet (CyberLab-Review)" -IPAddress 10.78.0.1 -PrefixLength 24','HOST • administrator PowerShell; run the final two lines only if not already configured')+p('Do not configure NAT, Internet Connection Sharing, a bridge, or a default gateway on this adapter. Keep Windows Firewall on. No inbound host service is required. This network is only for reviewing dummy data with patched tools; never move the Week 18 simulator or a vulnerable target here.')),
step('5. Understand the new VM profiles',p('Create ELASTIC01 in E2 (8 GB RAM, 4 vCPU, 80 GB disk) and SPLUNK01 in E4 (6 GB, 2 vCPU, 60 GB disk). Both use Ubuntu Server 24.04, installed using the existing Week 2 wizard. Download packages on Default Switch, then move the sole adapter to CyberLab-Review with static .40 or .41/24, no gateway, and no DNS. Install openssh-server, curl, nano, jq, unzip, and ca-certificates while online. Allow guest TCP 22 only from 10.78.0.1 before enabling ufw. Use console access for recovery. Browser interfaces will bind to guest loopback and travel through SSH tunnels.')+p('Security Onion uses its own 8 GB/4-vCPU/200 GB appliance and an explicitly documented Default Switch management exception in E5. Never run two SIEM appliances together on 16 GB. These are tiny training allocations, not vendor production reference sizing.')),
step('6. Prepare the file-transfer workflow',p('When a review guest is ready, verify its SSH host-key fingerprint in the guest console using sudo ssh-keygen -lf /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub. Compare it at first connection. From the host, use scp -r C:\\CyberLab\\Evidence\\case-m01 learner@10.78.0.40:~/ for Elastic, or substitute .41 for Splunk. A guest password is acceptable for initial isolated transfer; it must be unique. If adopting SSH keys, repeat Week 6’s key test before disabling password login. In the guest run cd ~/case-m01 followed by sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS. Every file must pass.'))],
['Original hash comparison and working-copy manifest','Written hypotheses and four planned queries','Internal-switch configuration and a power-on plan'],
['Why does this PCAP not prove the SSH login sequence?','How does an Internal switch differ from the original Private switch?'],
'Wrong extraction path: find README.txt before changing commands. Host cannot SSH: verify guest static IP, sole adapter, ssh service, and guest rule permitting .1. Do not enable a host inbound firewall rule to fix a guest SSH problem.',
[('Hyper-V networking','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/virtualization/hyper-v/plan/plan-hyper-v-networking-in-windows-server')])

add('ext-02-elastic','E2 · Build Elastic and ingest the common case','E1 and core Week 14. Docker is introduced below; core Week 20 is helpful but not required.',
'ELASTIC01 only: 8 GB RAM, 4 vCPU, 80 GB disk. All Wazuh, Windows, Splunk, and Security Onion guests off.',
'Elasticsearch indexes and searches documents; Kibana provides a browser interface. Logstash will transform events in E3. You will keep authentication and Elasticsearch TLS enabled, learn mappings explicitly, and verify both successful ingestion and a denied unauthenticated request. The lab uses the self-managed Basic license, not a cloud trial.',[
step('1. Build and patch the Ubuntu guest',p('Follow Week 2’s installation screens with VM ELASTIC01, hostname elastic01, and the allocation above. Use Default Switch to patch Ubuntu and install the tools from E1. Record the installed OS and kernel. Install the Ubuntu-packaged Docker engine; do not add learner to the docker group, which grants broad guest control.')+code('sudo apt update\nsudo apt upgrade\nsudo apt install docker.io openssh-server curl nano jq unzip ca-certificates\nsudo systemctl enable --now docker ssh\nsudo docker version','ELASTIC01 • Bash, update mode')),
step('2. Download one matching Elastic release',p('This edition follows the 9.5.3 images shown in the checked official documentation. Confirm all three tags exist and the release supports your platform before starting. Keep Elasticsearch, Kibana, and Logstash on the same patch version. If the mentor selects another version, change all three together and record it. Do not silently replace a version with latest.')+code('sudo docker pull docker.elastic.co/elasticsearch/elasticsearch:9.5.3\nsudo docker pull docker.elastic.co/kibana/kibana:9.5.3\nsudo docker pull docker.elastic.co/logstash/logstash:9.5.3\nsudo docker image ls --digests','ELASTIC01 • Bash, update mode')+p('Images are software templates; containers are running instances. Record image digests. Downloads may exceed the session estimate. Never start Elasticsearch while still in update mode.')),
step('3. Set the review address and guest controls',p('Shut down, move the sole adapter to CyberLab-Review, boot in the console, and use Week 3’s netplan procedure with 10.78.0.40/24, no gateway or DNS. Confirm ip route has no default route. Allow SSH from the host, then enable the firewall:')+code('sudo ufw default deny incoming\nsudo ufw default allow outgoing\nsudo ufw allow from 10.78.0.1 to any port 22 proto tcp\nsudo ufw enable\nsudo sysctl -w vm.max_map_count=1048576\nprintf "vm.max_map_count=1048576\\n" | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/90-cyberlab-elastic.conf','ELASTIC01 • Bash')+p('vm.max_map_count permits the memory mappings used by the index engine. Docker can bypass expected ufw treatment for published ports; the loopback binds below are the actual service-exposure boundary.')),
step('4. Start Elasticsearch with security intact',code('sudo docker network create elasticlab\nsudo docker volume create elasticlab-data\nsudo docker run -d -it --name es01 --network elasticlab -p 127.0.0.1:9200:9200 -m 3g -e discovery.type=single-node -e "ES_JAVA_OPTS=-Xms1g -Xmx1g" -v elasticlab-data:/usr/share/elasticsearch/data docker.elastic.co/elasticsearch/elasticsearch:9.5.3\nsudo docker logs --tail 40 es01','ELASTIC01 • Bash')+p('Create the network/volume/container once. The volume retains index data across container stops; keep this container too because its generated security configuration lives there. Wait until the service is ready, then set a unique administrator password interactively:')+code('sudo docker exec -it es01 /usr/share/elasticsearch/bin/elasticsearch-reset-password -u elastic -i\nsudo docker cp es01:/usr/share/elasticsearch/config/certs/http_ca.crt ./http_ca.crt\nsudo chmod 644 http_ca.crt\ncurl --cacert ./http_ca.crt -u elastic https://localhost:9200\ncurl --cacert ./http_ca.crt -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\\n" https://localhost:9200','ELASTIC01 • Bash')+p('curl -u elastic prompts for the password without putting it in command history. Expect authenticated cluster information and HTTP 401 without credentials. Do not disable security or use -k to make this pass. Store the password privately.')),
step('5. Enroll Kibana and open a narrow tunnel',code('sudo docker run -d --name kib01 --network elasticlab -p 127.0.0.1:5601:5601 -m 2g docker.elastic.co/kibana/kibana:9.5.3\nsudo docker exec -it es01 /usr/share/elasticsearch/bin/elasticsearch-create-enrollment-token -s kibana\nsudo docker exec kib01 /usr/share/kibana/bin/kibana-verification-code','ELASTIC01 • Bash')+p('Wait for Kibana startup before requesting its verification code. From host PowerShell start the tunnel below, then browse http://localhost:5601. Enter the enrollment token and verification code if prompted. Sign in as elastic. Tokens expire; regenerate if needed. The browser HTTP hop is local and the remote hop is encrypted by SSH; this is not a deployed HTTPS Kibana endpoint.')+code('ssh -N -L 5601:127.0.0.1:5601 learner@10.78.0.40','HOST • PowerShell')+p('Keep the tunnel window open. In Kibana Stack Management → License Management confirm Basic. Do not click Start trial. Run Test-NetConnection 10.78.0.40 -Port 5601 on the host: direct access should fail, while the tunnel works.')),
step('6. Create an explicit mapping',p('Kibana → search for Dev Tools → Console. Submit this request. One shard and zero replicas are for a single-node disposable lab, not high availability.')+code('PUT lab-case-m01\n{\n  "settings": {"number_of_shards": 1, "number_of_replicas": 0},\n  "mappings": {"properties": {\n    "@timestamp": {"type": "date"},\n    "event": {"properties": {"id": {"type": "keyword"}, "dataset": {"type": "keyword"}, "action": {"type": "keyword"}, "outcome": {"type": "keyword"}}},\n    "labels": {"properties": {"case_id": {"type": "keyword"}, "synthetic": {"type": "keyword"}}},\n    "source": {"properties": {"ip": {"type": "ip"}}},\n    "destination": {"properties": {"ip": {"type": "ip"}}},\n    "user": {"properties": {"name": {"type": "keyword"}}},\n    "url": {"properties": {"path": {"type": "keyword"}}},\n    "http": {"properties": {"response": {"properties": {"status_code": {"type": "integer"}}}}}\n  }}\n}','Kibana • Dev Tools request')+p('A mapping defines field types. An IP field supports address-aware operations; a keyword preserves exact values. Save the mapping in your notebook. Use the elastic administrator only for this private build; E6 designs a restricted analyst role.')),
step('7. Bulk-load and verify every item',p('Transfer case-m01 to ELASTIC01 with the E1 scp procedure and verify hashes. The bulk file contains alternating action/document lines, with stable IDs so repeating it replaces the same documents rather than doubling them.')+code('curl --cacert ~/http_ca.crt -u elastic -H "Content-Type: application/x-ndjson" --data-binary @/home/learner/case-m01/events.bulk.ndjson "https://localhost:9200/lab-case-m01/_bulk?refresh=true" > ~/case-m01/bulk-result.json\njq \'{errors,items:(.items|length)}\' ~/case-m01/bulk-result.json\ncurl --cacert ~/http_ca.crt -u elastic https://localhost:9200/lab-case-m01/_count','ELASTIC01 • Bash')+p('Expect errors:false, 16 item responses, and count:16. HTTP 200 alone does not prove each bulk operation succeeded. If errors:true, inspect the failing items before continuing.')),
step('8. Search, then close the session',p('Kibana → Stack Management → Data Views → Create data view. Name CASE-M01, pattern lab-case-m01, time field @timestamp. Open Discover, choose this view, and set an absolute interval covering 2026-09-01 09:59–10:03 UTC; account for Kibana’s displayed timezone or set dateFormat:tz to UTC in Advanced Settings. Run the KQL filter below.')+code('event.dataset : "linux.auth" and event.outcome : "failure"','Kibana • Discover KQL')+p('Expect six events. Inspect event.id, user.name, source.ip, and @timestamp. Search user.name:"nobody-in-this-case" and expect zero. Capture results, close the SSH tunnel, then sudo docker stop kib01 es01 and sudo poweroff. Do not remove the volume; E3 reuses it.'))],
['Authenticated success and unauthenticated 401','Basic license, mapped index, and all 16 successful bulk items','Six failed-login events and a zero-result negative query'],
['Why can an HTTP 200 bulk response contain failed documents?','Why is zero replicas acceptable only for this disposable single-node exercise?'],
'Container exited: inspect docker logs and docker inspect state, free -h, and disk space. Use docker start es01 kib01 on later sessions, not duplicate docker run commands. Enrollment failure: wait for ES health and generate a fresh Kibana token. Empty Discover: check data view and fixed historical time range.',
[('Elastic Docker setup','https://www.elastic.co/docs/deploy-manage/deploy/self-managed/install-elasticsearch-docker-basic'),('Docker production settings','https://www.elastic.co/docs/deploy-manage/deploy/self-managed/install-elasticsearch-docker-prod'),('Elastic subscription features','https://www.elastic.co/subscriptions'),('Bulk API','https://www.elastic.co/docs/api/doc/elasticsearch/operation/operation-bulk')])

add('ext-03-pipelines','E3 · Engineer a pipeline, detection, and retention policy','E2; keep ELASTIC01 and its containers/data volume.',
'ELASTIC01 only, 8 GB. Elasticsearch 3 GB container limit + Kibana 2 GB + temporary Logstash 1 GB; monitor host/guest pressure.',
'Log transport, parsing, field normalization, detection, and retention are separate engineering jobs. You will transform a differently shaped source into the same event model, reject a bad timestamp, and demonstrate why pipeline failures must be visible.',[
step('1. Resume and inspect source differences',code('sudo docker start es01 kib01\nhead -1 ~/case-m01/auth-input.ndjson\nhead -1 ~/case-m01/events.ndjson\nmkdir -p ~/elastic-pipeline','ELASTIC01 • Bash')+p('Reopen the SSH tunnel from E2. auth-input has flat source fields such as client_ip and result; events.ndjson has normalized nested fields. There are eight source authentication records. Do not ingest them into the existing 16-event index or your comparison counts will be misleading.')),
step('2. Build the Logstash transformation',p('Create ~/elastic-pipeline/normalize.conf with nano. This first pipeline writes to standard output only; it deliberately separates parsing from indexing. Docker will use stdin for these line-delimited events.')+code('input { stdin { codec => json } }\nfilter {\n  mutate {\n    rename => {\n      "event_id" => "[event][id]"\n      "client_ip" => "[source][ip]"\n      "username" => "[user][name]"\n      "result" => "[event][outcome]"\n      "case_id" => "[labels][case_id]"\n    }\n    add_field => {\n      "[event][dataset]" => "linux.auth"\n      "[event][action]" => "ssh_login"\n      "[labels][synthetic]" => "true"\n    }\n  }\n  date { match => ["event_time", "ISO8601"] target => "@timestamp" }\n  mutate { remove_field => ["event_time", "@version", "host"] }\n}\noutput { stdout { codec => json_lines } }','ELASTIC01 • normalize.conf')+p('Do not put a drop filter on parse failures. The date filter adds _dateparsefailure when parsing fails; those records must remain observable.')),
step('3. Validate and run the pipeline',code('sudo docker run --rm --network none -m 1g -e "LS_JAVA_OPTS=-Xms512m -Xmx512m" -v "$HOME/elastic-pipeline/normalize.conf:/usr/share/logstash/pipeline/normalize.conf:ro" docker.elastic.co/logstash/logstash:9.5.3 -f /usr/share/logstash/pipeline/normalize.conf --config.test_and_exit\nsudo docker run --rm -i --network none -m 1g -e "LS_JAVA_OPTS=-Xms512m -Xmx512m" -v "$HOME/elastic-pipeline/normalize.conf:/usr/share/logstash/pipeline/normalize.conf:ro" docker.elastic.co/logstash/logstash:9.5.3 -f /usr/share/logstash/pipeline/normalize.conf --log.level error --pipeline.workers 1 < ~/case-m01/auth-input.ndjson > ~/elastic-pipeline/output.txt','ELASTIC01 • Bash')+p('Logstash may emit startup text even with error logging. Extract only valid JSON event lines with the small script below; require exactly eight valid records and inspect any other lines. No network is needed because this stage only transforms files.')+code('import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path.home() / "elastic-pipeline"\nrows = []\nfor line in (p / "output.txt").read_text().splitlines():\n    try:\n        e = json.loads(line)\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        continue\n    if isinstance(e, dict) and isinstance(e.get("event"), dict) and e["event"].get("id"):\n        rows.append(e)\nassert len(rows) == 8, f"Expected 8 events, got {len(rows)}"\nassert all("_dateparsefailure" not in e.get("tags", []) for e in rows)\nwith (p / "auth.bulk.ndjson").open("w") as out:\n    for e in rows:\n        out.write(json.dumps({"index": {"_id": e["event"]["id"]}}) + "\\n")\n        out.write(json.dumps(e) + "\\n")\nprint("Prepared", len(rows), "normalized events")','ELASTIC01 • save as ~/elastic-pipeline/prepare.py, then run python3 ~/elastic-pipeline/prepare.py')),
step('4. Send validated output to a separate index',p('In Dev Tools create lab-auth-m01 using the same mapping request as E2, changing only the index name. Then submit the generated bulk file with curl:')+code('curl --cacert ~/http_ca.crt -u elastic -H "Content-Type: application/x-ndjson" --data-binary @/home/learner/elastic-pipeline/auth.bulk.ndjson "https://localhost:9200/lab-auth-m01/_bulk?refresh=true" > ~/elastic-pipeline/bulk-result.json\njq \'{errors,items:(.items|length)}\' ~/elastic-pipeline/bulk-result.json','ELASTIC01 • Bash')+p('Require eight successful items. Create a separate data view lab-auth-m01 with @timestamp. Compare IDs, original times, outcomes, and source IPs to the eight authentication records in lab-case-m01. This is a staged file pipeline; a continuously running collector/output is a later extension, not something this test proves.')),
step('5. Break the timestamp deliberately',p('Run the same Logstash command from step 3 with auth-bad.ndjson as input and output-bad.txt as destination. Inspect its emitted JSON and tags. Expect _dateparsefailure. Its fallback @timestamp is processing time, so a search in the historical case window may miss it. Keep that result outside your clean comparison indexes and record how you would quarantine and alert on it.')+p('Then in Dev Tools submit POST lab-auth-m01/_doc/bad-ip with body {"source":{"ip":"not-an-ip"}}. Expect a mapping-related HTTP 400. Verify _count remains eight. Delete is unnecessary because the invalid document should never have been indexed.')),
step('6. Define and replay a detection honestly',p('In Kibana Security → Rules/Detection rules → Create new rule, choose a Threshold rule. Set source index lab-case-m01, KQL event.dataset:"linux.auth" and event.outcome:"failure", group by source.ip and user.name, threshold 5. Name it LAB-M01 repeated failures. Set interval 1 minute and additional look-back 2 minutes. Keep external notifications/actions off. Save enabled. A successful rule save is not a successful detection test.')+p('The original events are historical. To test the current schedule, create six clearly labeled replay documents in lab-case-m01 using current UTC @timestamp values, IDs REPLAY-M01-01 through REPLAY-M01-06, and labels.case_id M01-REPLAY. Preserve original event time in an additional event.created field. Use current UTC from date -u in the guest; keep the six times within one minute. Wait two rule intervals, inspect the detection alert and group count, then disable the rule. If UI authorization/API-key errors occur, inspect the rule execution log. Document that this demonstrates replayed detection, not a historical alert arriving live.')+p('For a negative test, delete only the six REPLAY-M01 documents by their exact IDs, create four new current-time failure documents with a new case label M01-NEGATIVE and a different source IP 10.77.0.88, re-enable the rule, and verify no threshold alert for .88 after two intervals. Do not treat prior alerts for .12 as a new failure. Disable the rule afterward. Keep all original comparison queries scoped to labels.case_id:"CASE-M01".')),
step('7. Create a scoped retention policy',p('In Dev Tools submit these requests. The policy starts a seven-day delete phase from index lifecycle age; it is not seven days after each event timestamp. The template matches future lab-expiring-* indexes only. It does not apply to Wazuh or the comparison indexes.')+code('PUT _ilm/policy/lab-seven-day\n{"policy":{"phases":{"delete":{"min_age":"7d","actions":{"delete":{}}}}}}\n\nPUT _index_template/lab-expiring-template\n{"index_patterns":["lab-expiring-*"],"template":{"settings":{"index.lifecycle.name":"lab-seven-day","number_of_shards":1,"number_of_replicas":0}}}\n\nPUT lab-expiring-check\n\nGET lab-expiring-check/_ilm/explain','Kibana • Dev Tools, send each request separately')+p('Verify managed:true and the expected policy. Do not claim you observed seven-day deletion today. After recording evidence, delete only lab-expiring-check, the lab-expiring-template template, and lab-seven-day policy through their corresponding DELETE requests. Record a backup/export requirement before any real retention change.')),
step('8. Close and assess',p('Save configuration, positive/negative counts, the malformed-date result, mapping rejection, rule execution evidence, and retention explanation. Stop kib01 and es01, close the SSH tunnel, and shut down ELASTIC01. Do not run it alongside Splunk in E4.'))],
['Eight transformed events matching the canonical IDs/times','Observable malformed-date and invalid-IP failures','Threshold positive/negative replay plus scoped retention policy'],
['Why can a parsing failure become a misleading time-window problem?','How is an index-age retention policy different from per-event expiration?'],
'Logstash does not exit: verify redirected input reaches EOF; inspect output before interrupting. Missing JSON: check the bind-mounted filename and pipeline syntax. Rule does not fire: check enabled state, license-supported rule type, execution errors, source index, data timestamps, and group threshold. Never change the original fixture to hide a time problem.',
[('Logstash stdin','https://www.elastic.co/docs/reference/logstash/plugins/plugins-inputs-stdin'),('Date filter','https://www.elastic.co/docs/reference/logstash/plugins/plugins-filters-date'),('Threshold rules','https://www.elastic.co/docs/solutions/security/detect-and-alert/create-detection-rule'),('Index lifecycle management','https://www.elastic.co/docs/manage-data/lifecycle/index-lifecycle-management')])
